"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[4832],{34832:function(e,t,a){a.d(t,{ED:function(){return r},hI:function(){return n},Sh:function(){return l},_0:function(){return o}});var i=JSON.parse('[{"problemId":"design-url-shortener","slug":"design-url-shortener","problemType":"system-design","title":"Design a URL Shortener","prompt":"Design a URL shortening service similar to bit.ly or TinyURL. Users submit a long URL and receive a short alias (e.g., https://short.ly/aB3xY). When a user visits the short URL, the service redirects them to the original. The service must handle high read traffic, provide low-latency redirects, and allow optional analytics per short URL.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":35,"requirements":["Given a long URL, generate a unique short alias and return it to the user.","Given a short alias, resolve and redirect to the original URL with low latency.","Short aliases must be unique and collision-resistant.","Optionally support custom aliases chosen by the user.","Optionally record click count and basic analytics per short URL.","Short URLs should not expire by default; expiry is an optional configuration."],"constraints":["Write volume: ~100 million new URLs per day (assume 100:1 read-to-write ratio).","Redirect p99 latency target: under 10 ms.","Alias length: 7 characters (default) using [a-z, A-Z, 0-9] — 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion combinations.","Data retention: indefinite unless expiry is explicitly set.","Assume globally distributed read traffic, writes concentrated in a few regions."],"conceptSlugs":["caching","database-sharding","consistent-hashing","rate-limiting","database-indexing"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"url-shortener-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"Before designing, clarify the problem scope. What are the core functional requirements? What constraints and scale assumptions define the design space? Identify what is given, what you must assume, and what is explicitly out of scope.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":[],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Core requirements: generate short alias → store mapping → resolve + redirect. Key constraints: 100M writes/day ≈ 1,160 writes/sec; 100:1 read ratio ≈ 116,000 reads/sec; 62^7 alias space; p99 redirect < 10 ms. Out of scope for base design: per-user authentication, abuse prevention, real-time analytics dashboards.","tradeOffs":["Global read traffic favors edge caching or a distributed KV store over a single relational DB.","Custom aliases introduce collision risk and require reservation logic separate from the ID generation path."]}},{"stageId":"url-shortener-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Sketch your initial design. How would you generate short aliases? Where do you store the long-to-short mapping? How do you handle redirects at scale? Write your approach before revealing the reference solution.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing","caching"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"url-shortener-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review the reference architecture. Compare it to your approach — note where they align and where they differ.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching","database-sharding","database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Two-tier architecture: an ID generation service produces 7-character Base-62 aliases; a write path stores the mapping (shortId → longUrl, metadata) in a distributed KV store (e.g., DynamoDB or Cassandra); a read path resolves aliases via an in-memory cache (Redis) in front of the KV store and returns an HTTP 301/302 redirect.","approachSteps":["1. Client submits POST /shorten with the long URL.","2. API layer validates and rate-limits the request.","3. ID Generator creates a unique 7-char Base-62 alias (using a counter with Base-62 encoding or a hash-then-truncate strategy).","4. Write the mapping {shortId → longUrl, createdAt, userId?} to the KV store. Check uniqueness before committing.","5. Return the full short URL (https://short.ly/{alias}) to the client.","6. For redirect: client GET /alias → API checks Redis cache → on miss, reads KV store → returns 301 (permanent) or 302 (trackable) redirect.","7. Cache the mapping in Redis on first read (TTL-based). Hot URLs stay in cache indefinitely under TTL."],"diagramDescription":"Client → API Gateway → ID Generator (stateless) → KV Store (write); Client → CDN/Edge → Redis Cache → KV Store (read) → HTTP 3xx Redirect.","alternatives":["Hash-then-truncate (MD5/SHA256 of long URL, take first 7 chars): simpler but collision-prone; requires collision retry loop.","Centralized counter + Base-62 encode: globally unique but single-node bottleneck; mitigated with distributed counter partitioning.","UUID-based: collision-free but 36-char aliases — impractical for short links.","Geographically distributed counter ranges (Twitter Snowflake-style): eliminates bottleneck at cost of coordination overhead."]}},{"stageId":"url-shortener-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why 301 vs 302 redirect? Why Base-62 over UUID? What are the ID generation trade-offs? Walk through each key design decision and explain what it optimizes for.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching","database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Key decisions and their rationale.","tradeOffs":["301 (Permanent): Browser caches the redirect, reducing repeat load on the service. Cost: no click analytics on cached requests. Use when analytics are not needed.","302 (Temporary/Found): Every visit hits the service, enabling click counting. Cost: higher server load for popular links. Use when analytics are required.","Base-62 vs hash: Base-62 counter gives monotonically increasing, collision-free IDs at the cost of a coordination step. Hash-truncate is simpler but requires a collision retry mechanism.","Redis as redirect cache: Cold start adds one KV read per alias. Warm state handles virtually all read traffic without touching the persistent store — critical for the p99 < 10 ms target.","Custom aliases: Require a separate namespace to avoid collisions with generated IDs. Store in the same KV with a reserved prefix or a separate index."],"alternatives":["Skip caching (Redis) and read from KV directly: feasible for DynamoDB with sub-ms reads in the same region, but adds cross-region latency. Cache is recommended for global traffic.","Relational DB (Postgres) instead of KV: simpler query model and transactional custom-alias uniqueness checks. Bottleneck at 116,000 reads/sec requires read replicas + aggressive caching."]}},{"stageId":"url-shortener-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"What happens when a single short URL receives millions of hits in minutes (viral link)? How does your design handle ID exhaustion, cache failure, and regional outages?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching","rate-limiting","consistent-hashing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Handling hot links, exhaustion, and failure modes.","failureModes":["Hot/viral link: Single alias generates millions of cache reads. Redis handles this as a single key — but if that key evicts or Redis restarts, the KV store absorbs a thundering herd. Mitigation: never evict hot keys (LFU eviction policy + keep TTL long for popular links); use probabilistic early refresh.","ID space exhaustion: 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion — at 100M/day it takes ~95 years. Non-issue for MVP. Future: extend alias length to 8 chars (62^8 ≈ 218 trillion).","ID generator failure: If using a centralized counter, a single-node failure stops ID generation. Mitigation: replicate the counter with leader election (Zookeeper/etcd) or switch to a distributed range-based scheme.","Cache failure (Redis down): All redirects fall back to KV reads. At 116K reads/sec this can overwhelm KV. Mitigation: circuit breaker on the cache client; read replicas on the KV store; multi-layer edge cache (CDN) for the most popular links.","Regional outage: Cross-region replication of the KV store with eventual consistency. Reads from the nearest region. Writes: accept last-write-wins for duplicate short alias creation across regions (acceptable given the collision probability is extremely low)."]}},{"stageId":"url-shortener-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Changed constraint: the product team now requires real-time click analytics — total clicks, unique visitors (by IP hash), and referrer breakdown — accessible within 5 seconds of a click. How does this change your design? What new components do you introduce and what trade-offs arise?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Adding real-time analytics: event streaming path","approachSteps":["1. On each redirect request, publish a lightweight click event (shortId, timestamp, ipHash, referrer) to a Kafka topic asynchronously — do NOT block the redirect path.","2. A Flink or Kafka Streams job aggregates events over 5-second tumbling windows and writes counts to a time-series store (ClickHouse, DynamoDB with TTL, or Redis sorted sets).","3. An Analytics API reads from the aggregated store and serves dashboards.","4. To track unique visitors without storing PII, use HyperLogLog (Redis) for approximate cardinality — accurate to within 1% error."],"tradeOffs":["Async event emission on the redirect path: keeps redirect latency unaffected but risks losing click events if Kafka is unavailable. Accept at-least-once delivery with deduplication downstream.","Real-time window vs batch: 5-second windows require streaming infrastructure. Hourly batch (simpler) would not meet the requirement.","HyperLogLog for unique visitors: ~1% error is acceptable for analytics; exact counts require storing user identifiers, raising privacy concerns."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"design-rate-limiter","slug":"design-rate-limiter","problemType":"system-design","title":"Design a Distributed Rate Limiter","prompt":"Design a rate limiter for a public API that enforces per-user and per-IP request limits at scale. The service must handle high throughput, be deployable across multiple data center regions, and gracefully degrade when the rate-limiting backend is unavailable.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":30,"requirements":["Enforce per-user and per-IP rate limits (e.g., 1,000 requests/minute per user).","Return a clear 429 response with retry-after headers when limits are exceeded.","Support multiple limit windows (per-second, per-minute, per-hour).","The limiter must work across multiple API server instances.","Degrade gracefully: if the rate-limit store is unreachable, choose fail-open or fail-closed based on product policy."],"constraints":["API receives ~500,000 requests/second at peak.","Rate limit decisions must add ≤ 2 ms to request latency (p99).","The limiter must be consistent across 10+ API nodes in a region.","Configurable limits: different limits per API key tier (free, pro, enterprise).","No shared memory between API processes — distributed counter required."],"conceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","caching","consistent-hashing"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"rate-limiter-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"Identify the key requirements and constraints. What algorithms are candidates for rate limiting? What does \'distributed\' add to the problem compared to a single-server limiter?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Core tension: single-server counters don\'t work across nodes; distributed counters add network round-trips. The 2 ms latency budget is tight — a Redis call is typically 0.5–1 ms in the same data center. Algorithm choice (token bucket, fixed window, sliding window log, sliding window counter) affects accuracy, memory, and implementation complexity.","tradeOffs":["Fixed window: simple, low memory, but allows 2\xd7 burst at window boundaries.","Sliding window log: accurate, but memory grows with request count per user.","Sliding window counter (hybrid): accurate within ~0.5% error, O(1) memory — best for most production systems.","Token bucket: handles bursts naturally; refill rate maps to sustained limit."]}},{"stageId":"rate-limiter-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Choose an algorithm and sketch a design for the distributed rate limiter. How do API nodes share state? How do you handle latency and failure?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","caching"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"rate-limiter-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Compare your design to the sliding window counter with Redis. Note similarities and differences.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Sliding window counter backed by Redis. Each incoming request: compute window boundaries → INCRBY the current window counter for the key (userId:windowStart) → read previous window counter → compute weighted count = prevCount \xd7 (remaining fraction of prev window) + currCount → if weighted count > limit, return 429.","approachSteps":["1. Request arrives at API node with userId and timestamp.","2. Compute current 60-second window start: windowStart = floor(now / 60) * 60.","3. Redis MULTI: INCR user:{userId}:{windowStart}, GET user:{userId}:{windowStart-60}, EXPIRE user:{userId}:{windowStart} 120.","4. weightedCount = prevWindowCount \xd7 ((windowStart + 60 - now) / 60) + currWindowCount.","5. If weightedCount > limit: set X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0, return 429 with Retry-After header.","6. Else: decrement or allow — return 200 with X-RateLimit-Remaining header."],"alternatives":["Token bucket in Redis with Lua script: atomic, avoids race conditions, handles burst naturally. Slightly more complex Lua script to maintain.","Local in-memory limiter with gossip sync: low latency (no network call) but requires approximate counting — nodes can each allow up to limit/N individually, risking N\xd7 burst.","Centralized SQL counter: accurate but adds 5–20 ms per request — exceeds the 2 ms budget."]}},{"stageId":"rate-limiter-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why a sliding window over fixed window? Why Redis over a local in-memory counter? When would you choose fail-open vs fail-closed?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Decision rationale for key design choices.","tradeOffs":["Sliding window vs fixed window: Fixed window allows up to 2\xd7 burst at the boundary (last second of window N + first second of window N+1). Sliding window eliminates this at O(1) memory cost.","Redis vs local counter: Local is O(0ms) but each node allows limit requests independently — 10 nodes allow 10\xd7 limit. Redis adds ~1 ms but provides a shared accurate counter.","Fail-open (allow on Redis failure): Protects availability; risk: abuse during outage. Use for non-critical APIs or when abuse impact is low.","Fail-closed (block on Redis failure): Protects against abuse; risk: legitimate users blocked during outage. Use for billing/sensitive APIs.","Lua scripts for atomicity: Redis MULTI/EXEC provides optimistic locking; Lua scripts provide true atomicity. For rate limiting, a Lua script is preferred to avoid race between INCR and GET."]}},{"stageId":"rate-limiter-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"What happens during a Redis node failure? How do you handle hot users who generate millions of requests? How do you enforce limits for a user distributed across 5 geographic regions?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","consistent-hashing","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Redis node failure: With Redis Sentinel or Cluster, replica promotion takes 10–30 s. During that window: fail-open (allow) or local fallback counter (allow up to local_limit = limit/node_count). Redis Cluster with consistent hashing for key distribution — each userId is pinned to one shard.","Hot user: A single userId key in Redis becomes a hot key, concentrating load on one Redis shard. Mitigation: replicate hot keys to N replicas and read-balance across them; or shard the counter across K sub-keys (userId:0..K-1) and sum them — introduces K reads per request.","Cross-region rate limiting: Each region maintains its own Redis counter. Eventual synchronization via async replication — allows up to sum(regional_limits) globally. True global rate limiting requires a global counter service (high latency) or token-bucket with quota allocation per region."]}},{"stageId":"rate-limiter-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"New requirement: the API has 3 tiers — free (100 req/min), pro (1,000 req/min), enterprise (unlimited). Enterprise customers also get burst headroom of 50% for up to 10 seconds. How do you model this? Where does tier information come from?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["rate-limiting","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Tiered limits with burst allowance.","approachSteps":["1. Resolve tier from API key on each request — cache the key→tier mapping in local memory (5-minute TTL) to avoid DB round-trips.","2. Look up limit for the resolved tier from a config store (environment variable, feature flag service, or DB with in-process cache).","3. For enterprise burst: maintain a separate burst counter with a 10-second TTL. Allow up to limit * 1.5 for the burst window, then enforce normal limit.","4. Pass the effective limit into the sliding window counter logic — same algorithm, different threshold."],"tradeOffs":["Local API-key→tier cache: reduces DB load but has up to 5-minute lag on tier changes (acceptable for most plan upgrades).","Enterprise unlimited: skip the rate limiter entirely for unlimited-tier keys — reduces Redis calls. Risk: runaway client bugs consume infrastructure. Recommended: enforce a very high soft limit (e.g., 1M/min) and alert rather than truly unlimited."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"design-notification-service","slug":"design-notification-service","problemType":"system-design","title":"Design a Notification Service","prompt":"Design a notification service for a social media platform. The service must deliver notifications to users via push (mobile), email, and in-app channels. Notifications are triggered by user events (likes, comments, follows, mentions). The system must be reliable, handle notification preferences, and avoid spamming users.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":30,"requirements":["Deliver notifications via push (APNs/FCM), email (SMTP/SES), and in-app channels.","Respect per-user notification preferences (enabled/disabled per type and channel).","Deduplicate: do not send duplicate notifications for the same event within a time window.","Batch low-priority notifications (e.g., group 5 likes into one notification).","Prioritize: deliver time-sensitive notifications (e.g., DM, security alert) with low latency."],"constraints":["10 million daily active users; peak: 500,000 notification events/second.","Push delivery p99 < 5 seconds after event; email < 1 minute.","In-app notifications must be queryable (paginated history per user).","At-least-once delivery guarantee; tolerate duplicate delivery if deduplicated at receiver.","Support notification expiry (e.g., a \'like\' notification older than 24 hours is stale)."],"conceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","caching","database-sharding","rate-limiting"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"notification-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"Break down the core challenges: fanout (one event → many notifications), preference filtering, deduplication, and delivery guarantees. Identify the main components before designing.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Main challenges: (1) Fanout — a single event (e.g., a post from a celebrity with 10M followers) generates millions of push notifications. (2) Preference filtering — each recipient has different opt-in/opt-out settings. (3) Reliability — ensure delivery even if a channel (push) is temporarily down. (4) Batching/rate limiting — avoid overwhelming users with dozens of individual notifications.","tradeOffs":["Push fanout synchronously: simple but blocks event processing and slows the platform.","Message queue + async workers: decouples notification from event source; preferred pattern.","Write fanout vs read fanout: write fanout (write to each recipient\'s inbox on event) is simple but expensive for celebrities; read fanout (compute on read) reduces writes but increases read latency."]}},{"stageId":"notification-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Sketch the architecture. How does an event become a notification? How do you route to the right channel for each user? What ensures delivery?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","caching"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"notification-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review the event-driven fan-out architecture.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","caching","database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Event → Kafka topic → Notification Service (preference filter + dedup) → Channel Workers (push/email/in-app).","approachSteps":["1. Platform emits a domain event (e.g., UserLikedPost{postId, actorId, recipientId}) to Kafka.","2. Notification Service consumes the event: fetch recipient preferences from a low-latency cache (Redis); if the channel is disabled or event type is muted, drop the event.","3. Deduplication: check a Redis set {recipientId}:{eventType}:{entityId}:{windowHash} with TTL = dedup window (e.g., 1 hour). If key exists, drop; else SET NX and continue.","4. Batching worker: for low-priority types (likes, follows), accumulate events in a Redis sorted set by recipient, flush every 30 seconds, send a single batched notification.","5. Fanout: for high-priority (DM, security), route directly to the channel worker queue.","6. Channel workers: push worker calls APNs/FCM; email worker calls SES; in-app worker writes to a notifications table (sharded by userId) and updates a Redis badge counter.","7. Delivery failure: retry with exponential backoff; after max retries, write to a dead-letter queue for audit."],"diagramDescription":"Platform → Kafka(events) → NotificationService(filter+dedup) → [Push Queue → APNs/FCM] | [Email Queue → SES] | [InApp Worker → NotifTable(sharded)].","alternatives":["SNS (AWS): managed fan-out to SQS queues per channel. Reduces operational burden; less control over batching logic.","Celery/worker per recipient: simple for low scale; fan-out storm for popular users.","Websocket push for in-app: enables real-time updates without polling; requires persistent connection management."]}},{"stageId":"notification-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why separate queues per channel? Why Redis for dedup and preferences vs the primary DB? When would you use read fanout instead of write fanout?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"tradeOffs":["Separate queues per channel: failures in email delivery do not block push delivery; scaling is independent.","Redis for preferences: avoids DB read on every notification event; stale preference cache (up to TTL) is acceptable — a user muting a notification type will stop receiving after the TTL expires.","Write fanout vs read fanout: Write fanout writes to each follower\'s queue immediately — O(followers) writes per post. Read fanout computes on read — O(followers) reads per inbox load. Write fanout is faster for notification delivery; read fanout is cheaper for celebrities (99% of followers never check their inbox).","Hybrid: use write fanout for regular users (<10K followers) and read fanout (or dedicated celebrity workers with throttled write) for high-follower accounts."]}},{"stageId":"notification-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"A celebrity with 5M followers posts a video. Push notifications need to go out within 5 seconds. How does your design handle this fanout burst? What happens if APNs is slow or down?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","rate-limiting"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Celebrity fanout: 5M push notifications in 5 seconds = 1M/sec. A single Kafka consumer + push worker cannot keep up. Solution: horizontal scaling of push workers; partition the notifications Kafka topic by userId hash so consumers can parallelize. Precompute follower lists for high-fan-out accounts.","APNs slow: push workers timeout waiting for APNs. Solution: set a short timeout (2 s); if APNs is slow, put undelivered notifications back in the queue with lower priority. Users will receive the push when APNs recovers.","APNs down: retry with exponential backoff up to the notification expiry TTL. After expiry, drop — stale notifications degrade UX more than missing ones.","In-app notification write surge: shard the notifications table by userId; use a write-behind cache (Redis) to absorb burst writes and flush to the DB asynchronously."]}},{"stageId":"notification-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"New requirement: users can now set a \'Do Not Disturb\' schedule (e.g., no notifications from 10 PM to 8 AM local time). High-priority notifications (security alerts, DMs) must still be delivered immediately. How do you implement this without blocking the notification pipeline?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"DND scheduling with priority override.","approachSteps":["1. Store DND schedules in the user preferences table (start_hour, end_hour, timezone).","2. When a notification is about to be dispatched, check if recipient is in DND window (convert user\'s local time using stored timezone).","3. If DND active AND notification priority is not \'critical\': delay the notification by adding it to a scheduled delivery queue (Redis sorted set keyed by userId, scored by scheduled_delivery_time).","4. A scheduler process polls the sorted set every minute and re-enqueues notifications whose delivery time has passed.","5. If priority is \'critical\' (DM, security): bypass DND check and deliver immediately."],"tradeOffs":["Redis sorted set for delayed delivery: simple, low latency, but Redis is in-memory — persisted scheduled notifications need DB backup for durability.","Per-notification vs batch: check DND per notification at dispatch time. More accurate than batch processing, but adds a Redis lookup per notification.","Timezone accuracy: always store user timezone explicitly — do not infer from IP. Use IANA timezone identifiers."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"lru-cache-implementation","slug":"lru-cache-implementation","problemType":"low-level-design","title":"Implement an LRU Cache","prompt":"Design a data structure that follows the constraints of a Least Recently Used (LRU) cache. Implement get(key) and put(key, value) operations in O(1) average time complexity. When the cache reaches its capacity, it should invalidate and evict the least recently used key before inserting a new item.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":25,"requirements":["Initialize LRUCache with positive capacity.","int get(int key) returns the value of the key if it exists, otherwise -1.","void put(int key, int value) updates the value of the key if it exists. Otherwise, add the key-value pair to the cache. If keys exceed capacity, evict least recently used key.","Both get and put operations must run in O(1) average time complexity."],"constraints":["1 <= capacity <= 3000","0 <= key <= 10^4","0 <= value <= 10^5","At most 2 * 10^5 calls will be made to get and put."],"conceptSlugs":["caching","database-indexing"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"lru-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"What makes O(1) get and put tricky together? What data structure gives O(1) lookup? What data structure gives O(1) removal and insertion at arbitrary positions?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Hash map provides O(1) key lookup, but doesn\'t track ordering. Array tracks order, but eviction/shifting is O(N). Doubly linked list allows O(1) node deletion and insertion when a pointer is held. Combining a Hash Map with a Doubly Linked List achieves O(1) for both get and put.","tradeOffs":["Hash Map + Doubly Linked List: O(1) operations at cost of 2 pointers per node + hash map entry memory overhead.","Array-based cache: lower memory overhead due to cache locality, but O(N) shifts on update/evict make it unsuitable for high throughput."]}},{"stageId":"lru-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Sketch your design. What does each Node contain? What are the dummy head and tail nodes for? Write pseudo-code for get and put before revealing the solution.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"lru-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Inspect the reference implementation. How are pseudo head and tail used to eliminate boundary null checks?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Use a Hash Map mapping key -> Node, and a doubly linked list with dummy head and tail nodes. Most recently used nodes are placed near head, least recently used are evicted from near tail.","approachSteps":["1. Node contains key, value, prev, next pointers.","2. Dummy head and tail nodes avoid special cases for empty list and single-item updates.","3. get(key): if key in map, find node, removeNode(node), addNodeToHead(node), return node.val; else return -1.","4. put(key, val): if key exists, update val, move to head. If new, create node, add to head, insert into map. If size > capacity, remove tail.prev node, delete from map."],"alternatives":["Singly Linked List: cannot delete an arbitrary node in O(1) because previous pointer is unknown.","LinkedHashMap / OrderedDict: language-provided built-in, but understanding the underlying pointer manipulation is required in engineering interviews."]}},{"stageId":"lru-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why store key in Node in addition to value? What happens during eviction if Node only stores value?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Trade-offs in LRU Cache design.","tradeOffs":["Storing key in Node: enables O(1) removal from the hash map when evicting tail.prev node (map.delete(tailNode.key)). Without key in Node, deleting from hash map would require an O(N) search through the map.","Dummy Head & Tail: eliminates edge checks for head === null or tail === null in add and remove methods, drastically reducing bug surface."]}},{"stageId":"lru-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"What happens when multiple threads execute get and put concurrently? How does coarse-grained locking vs segmented locking affect throughput?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Concurrent access race conditions: Multiple threads mutating doubly-linked list pointers cause cyclic references and pointer corruption.","Coarse-grained lock (ReentrantLock / Mutex): thread-safe, but serializes all reads and writes, creating CPU bottleneck under high concurrent read traffic.","Read-write lock: allows concurrent reads, but in LRU every read (get) mutates the list to promote the accessed node, turning every read into a write lock! Solution: Read-Write lock with buffered access queues (like Caffeine Cache) or Clock/Second-Chance approximation."]}},{"stageId":"lru-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Follow-up: How would you implement Time-To-Live (TTL) expiration alongside LRU eviction? Does expired item cleanup run on get, or in a background cleaner?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"TTL combined with LRU eviction.","approachSteps":["1. Add expiresAt timestamp to each Node.","2. Passive expiration: on get(key), check if now > node.expiresAt. If expired, evict immediately and return -1.","3. Active expiration: periodic background task samples keys or inspects a min-heap ordered by expiresAt to reclaim memory proactively."],"tradeOffs":["Passive only: low overhead, but dead keys that are never read again waste memory until LRU capacity eviction forces them out.","Active with Min-Heap: proactive memory reclamation, but adding/updating keys requires O(log N) heap adjustments instead of strict O(1)."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"top-k-frequent-elements","slug":"top-k-frequent-elements","problemType":"coding-algorithm","title":"Top K Frequent Elements","prompt":"Given an integer array nums and an integer k, return the k most frequent elements. The algorithm\'s time complexity must be better than O(N log N), where N is the array\'s size.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":20,"requirements":["Return the k most frequent elements in any order.","Time complexity must be strictly better than O(N log N).","Handle negative numbers and arrays with all identical elements.","k is always valid (1 <= k <= number of unique elements)."],"constraints":["1 <= nums.length <= 10^5","-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4","k is in the range [1, the number of unique elements in the array].","It is guaranteed that the answer is unique."],"conceptSlugs":["database-indexing","caching"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"top-k-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"Why does full sorting take O(N log N)? What approaches avoid full sorting? Compare min-heap vs bucket sort.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"First pass: frequency map takes O(N) time and O(U) space where U is unique elements. Full sort on frequencies is O(U log U). Min-heap of size k is O(N log k). Bucket sort where index represents frequency is O(N) linear time.","tradeOffs":["Min-heap: O(N log k) time, O(N) space. Works on streaming data where total size N is unknown ahead of time.","Bucket sort: O(N) time, O(N) space. Requires finite bounded frequency space (max frequency is N)."]}},{"stageId":"top-k-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Write your approach. How will you store element counts? How will you extract the top k efficiently?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"top-k-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review both Min-Heap and Bucket Sort reference solutions.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Bucket Sort approach delivers optimal O(N) time complexity.","approachSteps":["1. Count occurrences of each number using a HashMap: freqMap[num] = count.","2. Create an array of buckets of size N+1, where bucket[i] holds numbers that appear i times.","3. Populate buckets: iterate through freqMap and append num to bucket[freqMap[num]].","4. Iterate from bucket[N] down to bucket[0], collecting numbers into result array until result.length === k."],"alternatives":["Quickselect: partition elements based on frequency until pivot is at index N-k. Average O(N), worst-case O(N^2).","Min-Heap of size k: push [freq, num]; if heap.size > k, pop minimum. O(N log k)."]}},{"stageId":"top-k-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"When would you choose a Min-Heap over Bucket Sort in real production systems?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"tradeOffs":["Streaming input: In a live telemetry stream (e.g., top trending search queries), N is infinite. Bucket sort requires an end boundary. A Min-Heap or Count-Min Sketch operates continuously on unbounded streams.","Memory locality: Bucket sort creates an array of lists, causing memory fragmentation if U is sparse."]}},{"stageId":"top-k-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"Scale challenge: The input contains 10 billion search queries per day across 500 servers. How do you find the global Top K queries without sending all queries to a single machine?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing","caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Single machine memory exhaustion: 10 billion queries cannot fit in RAM for a full HashMap count.","Distributed MapReduce / Spark: Map step computes local counts per partition; Shuffle partitions by query hash; Reduce aggregates global counts; final stage runs Top-K heap per reducer.","Probabilistic streaming (Heavy Hitters): Use Count-Min Sketch + Min-Heap (Space-Saving algorithm) on each edge node, then merge sketches at the aggregator with bounded error guarantees."]}},{"stageId":"top-k-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Follow-up: What if k = 10,000 and queries arrive with timestamps, and we need the Top K queries within the last 15-minute sliding window?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["caching"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Sliding window top-k aggregation.","approachSteps":["1. Divide 15-minute window into 15 one-minute buckets.","2. Compute Top-K sketch per minute bucket.","3. On window shift, drop the oldest bucket and sum counts from the remaining 14 buckets plus active bucket.","4. Query time: merge 15 bucket sketches and extract top K."],"tradeOffs":["Bucket granularity: 1-minute buckets give maximum 1-minute lag on eviction at low overhead. 1-second buckets increase precision at 60x memory cost."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"distributed-unique-id-generator","slug":"distributed-unique-id-generator","problemType":"system-design","title":"Design a Distributed Unique ID Generator","prompt":"Design a globally distributed unique ID generation service (similar to Twitter Snowflake). Generated IDs must be 64-bit integers, globally unique, roughly time-sortable, and generated at high scale without cross-node network coordination.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":30,"requirements":["IDs must be globally unique across all nodes.","IDs must fit into a 64-bit integer (int64) for database index efficiency.","IDs must be roughly chronological / time-sortable.","High availability: service must generate at least 10,000 IDs per second per node with sub-millisecond latency.","No single point of failure and no runtime consensus round-trips (Paxos/Raft) in the ID generation path."],"constraints":["64 bits maximum.","System lifetime: at least 50 years before epoch overflow.","Clock synchronization: tolerate minor NTP clock drift without generating duplicate IDs."],"conceptSlugs":["database-sharding","consistency-replication"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"id-gen-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"Why are UUID v4 unsuitable for high-throughput primary keys? Why is a centralized auto-increment database counter a bottleneck?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"UUID v4 is 128-bit and non-sequential, causing B-tree fragmentation, page splits, and cache misses in database indexes. Centralized DB auto-increment introduces a single write bottleneck and single point of failure.","tradeOffs":["UUID v4: fully distributed, zero coordination, but 128-bit non-sequential degrades DB indexing.","Centralized DB counter (Ticket Server): simple and sequential, but single point of failure and latency bottleneck.","Snowflake 64-bit scheme: 64-bit, time-sortable, decentralized, but depends on system clock stability."]}},{"stageId":"id-gen-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Design the 64-bit bit layout. How many bits for timestamp, machine ID, and sequence number? How do you prevent collisions within the same millisecond?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"id-gen-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review Twitter Snowflake 64-bit layout.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-sharding","consistency-replication"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Twitter Snowflake 64-bit structure: 1 sign bit + 41 timestamp bits + 10 machine/datacenter bits + 12 sequence bits.","approachSteps":["1. 1 unused sign bit (always 0 to keep integer positive).","2. 41 timestamp bits: milliseconds since custom epoch. 2^41 ms ≈ 69.7 years of IDs.","3. 10 node ID bits: 5 bits datacenter ID (32 datacenters) + 5 bits worker ID (32 workers per DC) = 1,024 generator nodes.","4. 12 sequence bits: supports up to 2^12 = 4,096 IDs per millisecond per node.","5. Algorithm: read current timestamp. If same millisecond as last ID, increment sequence. If sequence overflows 4095, busy-wait until next millisecond. If new millisecond, reset sequence to 0."],"diagramDescription":"[1 bit: Sign][41 bits: Millisecond Timestamp][10 bits: Machine ID][12 bits: Sequence Number]","alternatives":["Ticket Server with multi-master offsets: Node A generates odd IDs, Node B generates even IDs. Simple, but does not scale dynamically and doesn\'t time-sort cleanly.","ULID (Universally Unique Lexicographically Sortable Identifier): 128-bit time-sortable alternative, useful when 64-bit constraint is relaxed."]}},{"stageId":"id-gen-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why use a custom epoch instead of Unix epoch (1970)? What are the machine ID assignment trade-offs?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":{"tradeOffs":["Custom epoch: Starting timestamp at application launch year (e.g., 2026) gives 69 years of runtime from now. Unix epoch would overflow 41 bits in 2039.","Static worker ID assignment vs ZooKeeper/etcd: Static config is simple but prone to human config error (duplicate worker IDs). Zookeeper/etcd ephemeral nodes assign unique worker IDs automatically on startup."]}},{"stageId":"id-gen-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"Clock drift disaster: An NTP sync causes the server\'s system clock to move backward by 100 milliseconds. What happens if the generator continues issuing IDs? How do you defend against it?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistency-replication"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Clock skew / rollback: If system clock jumps backward, the generator will produce duplicate timestamps and therefore duplicate IDs!","Defense 1 (Wait): If clock rollback is small (< 5 ms), spin-wait until the clock catches up to lastTimestamp.","Defense 2 (Reject): If clock rollback is significant, reject requests and raise an alert; refuse to generate IDs until operator verifies or clock catches up.","Defense 3 (Clock-sequence bits): Reserve 2 bits for clock sequence counter; increment clock sequence when rollback is detected to maintain uniqueness."]}},{"stageId":"id-gen-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Follow-up: What if client requests peak above 4,096 IDs in a single millisecond on one node? How does the generator absorb this burst?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Sequence saturation handling.","approachSteps":["1. When sequence reaches 4,095, block and yield CPU until timestamp advances to the next millisecond.","2. Horizontal scale: add more generator worker nodes to distribute the load across more 10-bit machine namespaces."],"tradeOffs":["Blocking on sequence overflow: introduces microsecond latency jitter on burst, but guarantees strict uniqueness without allocating extra bits."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"thread-safe-bounded-blocking-queue","slug":"thread-safe-bounded-blocking-queue","problemType":"low-level-design","title":"Design a Thread-Safe Bounded Blocking Queue","prompt":"Design and implement a thread-safe Bounded Blocking Queue with put(element) and get() methods. When the queue is full, producer threads calling put() must block until space becomes available. When the queue is empty, consumer threads calling get() must block until an element is added.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":25,"requirements":["Bounded capacity specified at construction time.","put(element): inserts element, blocking if full until space is available.","get(): retrieves and removes head element, blocking if empty until element is available.","Thread safety: safe under concurrent access by multiple producer and consumer threads.","FIFO ordering: elements are retrieved in the order they were inserted."],"constraints":["No busy-waiting / polling loops (while true sleep). Must use condition synchronization.","Guard against spurious wakeups.","Prevent deadlocks between producers and consumers."],"conceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture","caching"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"queue-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"What is the difference between a mutex lock and a condition variable? Why is a single mutex insufficient without condition variables?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"A mutex provides mutual exclusion (only one thread enters critical section). Condition variables allow threads to release the mutex and sleep until signaled that state has changed. We need two conditions: notEmpty (consumers wait on) and notFull (producers wait on).","tradeOffs":["One lock + two condition variables: clean, thread-safe, prevents deadlocks.","Two locks (head lock & tail lock like Java LinkedBlockingQueue): allows simultaneous enqueue and dequeue, boosting concurrency at cost of complex state tracking."]}},{"stageId":"queue-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Sketch your class implementation with put and get. Why must condition checks use while loops instead of if statements?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"queue-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review the reference implementation with circular array and condition variables.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Circular buffer backed by an array, guarded by a ReentrantLock with notFull and notEmpty Condition instances.","approachSteps":["1. State: array items, head index, tail index, count, capacity, lock, notFull condition, notEmpty condition.","2. put(item): lock.lock() -> while (count === capacity) notFull.await() -> items[tail] = item -> tail = (tail + 1) % capacity -> count++ -> notEmpty.signal() -> lock.unlock().","3. get(): lock.lock() -> while (count === 0) notEmpty.await() -> item = items[head] -> head = (head + 1) % capacity -> count-- -> notFull.signal() -> return item -> lock.unlock()."],"alternatives":["Lock-free circular ring buffer (Disruptor pattern): uses atomic CAS (compare-and-swap) and memory barriers to achieve millions of ops/sec without mutex overhead.","Semaphore-based implementation: two counting semaphores for items and spaces, plus a binary semaphore for mutex."]}},{"stageId":"queue-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why use \'while (count === capacity)\' instead of \'if (count === capacity)\'? Explain spurious wakeups.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Condition synchronization invariants.","tradeOffs":["Spurious wakeups: OS threads can wake from await() without an explicit signal. Using a while loop re-checks the predicate after wakeup, guaranteeing safety.","Multiple waiters: If signal() wakes multiple threads or another producer acquires lock first, the queue might be full again by the time the thread acquires the lock. The while loop catches this."]}},{"stageId":"queue-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"Thread starvation: What happens if 100 producer threads are constantly waiting to insert and consumers process slowly? How does a fair lock vs non-fair lock impact latency and throughput?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Thread starvation with non-fair locks: Newly arrived threads can steal the lock from waiting threads, causing long-tail latency for waiting threads.","Fair Lock (FIFO acquisition): Guarantees no thread starves, but throughput drops significantly (up to 4x slower) due to context switches.","SignalAll thundering herd: Calling signalAll() wakes all waiting threads, but only one acquires the lock while the rest sleep again. Always use signal() unless broadcasting a state reset."]}},{"stageId":"queue-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Follow-up: How would you add a timeout to put (e.g. put(item, timeoutMillis)) to prevent indefinite producer blocking?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["event-driven-architecture"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Timeout-based await pattern.","approachSteps":["1. Use condition.awaitNanos(nanosTimeout) inside the while loop.","2. Track remaining time: nanosTimeout = condition.awaitNanos(nanosTimeout).","3. If nanosTimeout <= 0 and count === capacity, return false (timed out)."],"tradeOffs":["Timeout protection: Prevents resource deadlock in microservices when downstream consumers freeze."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"design-key-value-store","slug":"design-key-value-store","problemType":"system-design","title":"Design a Distributed Key-Value Store","prompt":"Design a distributed, highly available key-value store similar to DynamoDB or Cassandra. The system must support put(key, value) and get(key) across multiple storage nodes with configurable consistency, automatic data partitioning, and failure recovery.","difficulty":"hard","expectedEffortMinutes":40,"requirements":["put(key, value): store or update key-value pair.","get(key): retrieve value associated with key.","High availability and fault tolerance: survive node failures without data loss.","Horizontal scalability: scale storage and throughput by adding nodes.","Tunable consistency: support configurable R (read quorum) and W (write quorum)."],"constraints":["Key size: up to 256 bytes; value size: up to 1 MB.","Low latency: p99 read and write under 10 ms within same datacenter.","Handle network partitions (CAP theorem AP system with eventual consistency)."],"conceptSlugs":["consistent-hashing","database-sharding","consistency-replication"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"kv-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"What makes a distributed KV store fundamentally different from a single Redis instance? How do consistent hashing, replication, and quorum consistency work together?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistent-hashing","database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Distributed KV stores partition data across nodes using Consistent Hashing, replicate each key across N nodes, and use quorum voting (R + W > N) for strong consistency or eventual consistency.","tradeOffs":["Consistent Hashing vs Range Partitioning: Consistent hashing distributes write load evenly without hotspots; range partitioning enables efficient range scans but causes write hotspots on sequential keys.","Sloppy Quorum + Hinted Handoff vs Strict Quorum: Sloppy quorum maintains write availability during network partition, but risks stale reads until hinted handoff reconciles."]}},{"stageId":"kv-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Sketch the architecture. How does a coordinator node handle put and get? How are replicas selected on the consistent hash ring?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistent-hashing"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"kv-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review Dynamo-style architecture components: Hash Ring, Coordinator, LSM-Tree, and Gossip Protocol.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistent-hashing","database-sharding","consistency-replication"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Dynamo-style decentralized architecture with Consistent Hashing ring and virtual nodes.","approachSteps":["1. Consistent Hash Ring: Map key to ring position using hash(key). The first N distinct physical nodes clockwise from the key position store replicas.","2. Virtual Nodes (tokens): Each physical node owns V virtual positions on the ring to distribute data uniformly.","3. Write Path: Coordinator sends write to all N replicas. Waits for W ACKs before returning success. On local node: write to Commit Log (WAL) -> MemTable in RAM -> flush to SSTables on disk (LSM-Tree).","4. Read Path: Coordinator requests data from R replicas. Compares version timestamps/vector clocks. Returns most recent version and triggers read repair asynchronously."],"alternatives":["Master-Slave (Leader-Follower): Single leader coordinates all writes. Simple consistency, but leader failover causes write downtime.","B-Tree storage engine: in-place disk updates cause random I/O; LSM-Trees turn random writes into sequential writes, making LSM superior for high write throughput."]}},{"stageId":"kv-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why configure R + W > N? What happens if R=1, W=1, N=3? How are write conflicts resolved?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistency-replication"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Quorum configurations and conflict resolution.","tradeOffs":["R + W > N (Strong Consistency): At least one node in read quorum overlaps with write quorum, guaranteeing the latest value is read.","W=1, R=1, N=3 (Eventual Consistency / Fast): Low latency and high availability, but high risk of reading stale data.","Last-Write-Wins (LWW) vs Vector Clocks: LWW uses wall-clock timestamps (simple, but clock drift can overwrite valid concurrent writes). Vector Clocks preserve concurrent versions for client reconciliation, but add metadata size."]}},{"stageId":"kv-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"Failure handling: A storage node crashes for 2 hours and restarts. How does it catch up on missed writes without downloading the entire database?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistency-replication"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Hinted Handoff: During node outage, neighboring nodes store writes tagged with a hint. When the node recovers, neighbors deliver the hinted writes.","Anti-Entropy with Merkle Trees: Nodes compare hierarchical hash trees (Merkle Trees) of their key ranges. Only branches with differences are synchronized across the network, minimizing transfer bandwidth.","Gossip Protocol failure detection: Nodes exchange heartbeats randomly with neighbors. If a node fails to heartbeat within timeout, consensus marks it down without central health monitor."]}},{"stageId":"kv-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Follow-up: How do you handle Hot Partitions where a single celebrity key receives 100,000 writes/sec?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["consistent-hashing","database-sharding"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Hot key mitigation.","approachSteps":["1. Salting: append random suffix (key_0 to key_K) to scatter writes across multiple ring partitions.","2. Read aggregation: read all K salted keys and merge values.","3. In-memory write buffering: absorb writes in an edge cache (Redis) and flush to KV store in batch."],"tradeOffs":["Salting increases write throughput for hot keys, but scatters reads across K queries."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"circuit-breaker-resilience","slug":"circuit-breaker-resilience","problemType":"design-pattern","title":"Design a Circuit Breaker for Microservices","prompt":"Design a resilient Circuit Breaker pattern to protect distributed microservices from cascading failures when downstream dependencies degrade or fail. The breaker must support Closed, Open, and Half-Open states with automatic recovery testing.","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":25,"requirements":["Track failure rates and slow call rates over a sliding evaluation window.","Transition from Closed to Open when failure threshold is exceeded, failing fast immediately without calling downstream.","Transition from Open to Half-Open after a configured sleep window, allowing a trial percentage of probe calls.","Transition from Half-Open to Closed if probes succeed, or back to Open if probes fail.","Provide fallback responses when circuit is Open."],"constraints":["Minimal overhead on the critical request path (< 0.1 ms latency added).","Thread-safe state transitions without blocking concurrent in-flight requests."],"conceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker","rate-limiting"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"cb-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"What is cascading failure in microservices? Why does an upstream service calling a slow downstream service exhaust thread pools?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"When Service B slows down, Service A\'s worker threads block waiting for responses. Soon Service A runs out of threads and fails to serve its own clients, cascading the failure through the entire system. Circuit Breakers fail fast immediately, preventing thread pool exhaustion.","tradeOffs":["Fail-Fast vs Persistent Retries: Retrying a struggling service creates a retry storm that worsens the outage. Circuit breaker stops calls completely to allow downstream recovery."]}},{"stageId":"cb-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Draw the finite state machine: Closed, Open, Half-Open. What transitions trigger each state change?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"cb-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review the state machine implementation and sliding window metrics.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"State machine with Count-based or Time-based sliding window metrics.","approachSteps":["1. Closed: Calls pass to downstream. Record call outcomes (success, failure, timeout). If failureRate > threshold (e.g. 50% over last 100 calls), trip to Open.","2. Open: Reject incoming calls immediately with CircuitBreakerOpenException or execute fallback. Start waitDuration timer (e.g. 30 seconds).","3. Half-Open: When timer expires, enter Half-Open. Allow a limited number of test calls (e.g. 10 probe calls).","4. If probe failureRate < threshold: reset to Closed. If probe fails: return to Open for another waitDuration."],"diagramDescription":"[Closed] --(failure > 50%)--> [Open] --(timer expired)--> [Half-Open] --(probes pass)--> [Closed] OR --(probes fail)--> [Open]","alternatives":["Simple timeout + retry with exponential backoff: handles transient network blips, but does not stop cascading thread exhaustion.","Rate limiting: limits request volume, but doesn\'t adapt dynamically to downstream health."]}},{"stageId":"cb-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Sliding window trade-off: Count-based window vs Time-based window. Which is better for low-traffic services vs high-traffic services?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker"],"referenceMaterial":{"tradeOffs":["Count-based (last N calls): Good for predictable call volumes, but in low-traffic periods 5 failures over 2 days could trip the circuit.","Time-based (last T seconds): Reflects current operational health accurately, but requires minimum call threshold before evaluation to avoid tripping on a single failure."]}},{"stageId":"cb-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"Distributed Circuit Breaker: When you have 500 API instances calling a downstream service, should circuit state be shared across instances (Redis) or local per instance?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker","rate-limiting"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Distributed state (Redis): High precision global state, but adds network latency to every call and creates a dependency on Redis. If Redis is down, circuit breakers fail!","Local in-memory state: Zero latency overhead, highly resilient. Downstream receives at most 500 * probe_count test calls, which is typically safe for downstream capacity."]}},{"stageId":"cb-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Follow-up: How do you design meaningful Fallback mechanisms for payments vs recommendation feeds?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["circuit-breaker"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Contextual fallback strategies.","approachSteps":["1. Recommendations / Feeds: Return cached recommendations or default popular items. Degradation is invisible to user.","2. Search: Fall back to basic keyword search instead of vector neural search.","3. Payments / Checkout: NEVER fake a payment. Return clean error advising user to retry shortly, or queue transaction for asynchronous background processing."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]},{"problemId":"course-schedule-cycle-detection","slug":"course-schedule-cycle-detection","problemType":"coding-algorithm","title":"Course Schedule & Cycle Detection","prompt":"There are numCourses courses you have to take, labeled from 0 to numCourses - 1. You are given an array prerequisites where prerequisites[i] = [a, b] indicates that you must take course b before course a. Determine if you can finish all courses (i.e. check if the prerequisite graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph / DAG).","difficulty":"medium","expectedEffortMinutes":20,"requirements":["Return true if all courses can be finished, false if there is a circular dependency.","Prerequisites graph can be disconnected (multiple components).","Graph can contain self-loops or duplicate edges.","Time complexity must be O(V + E) where V is numCourses and E is prerequisites.length."],"constraints":["1 <= numCourses <= 2000","0 <= prerequisites.length <= 5000","prerequisites[i].length == 2","0 <= ai, bi < numCourses"],"conceptSlugs":["database-indexing","caching"],"reviewState":"canonical","stages":[{"stageId":"course-understand","stageKind":"understand","learnerPrompt":"Model the problem as a graph. What are vertices and edges? What condition makes it impossible to complete all courses?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Directed graph where vertices are courses and directed edge (b -> a) represents dependency. If the graph contains a directed cycle (e.g. A -> B -> C -> A), no course in the cycle can ever be taken first, making completion impossible.","tradeOffs":["Kahn\'s Algorithm (BFS with In-degrees): Natural and iterative; directly produces topological order.","DFS with 3-state coloring: Recursion-based; detects cycles efficiently in memory."]}},{"stageId":"course-attempt","stageKind":"attempt","learnerPrompt":"Choose Kahn\'s algorithm (BFS) or DFS with coloring. Write your approach to detect cycles.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":null},{"stageId":"course-solution","stageKind":"solution","learnerPrompt":"Review Kahn\'s Algorithm (Topological Sort via BFS).","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Kahn\'s Algorithm (BFS Topological Sort) using in-degree array.","approachSteps":["1. Build adjacency list adj and in-degree array inDegree of size numCourses.","2. For each [dest, src] in prerequisites: adj[src].push(dest), inDegree[dest]++.","3. Enqueue all courses with inDegree === 0 into a queue (courses with no prerequisites).","4. While queue is not empty: dequeue course u, increment completedCount, and for each neighbor v in adj[u], decrement inDegree[v]. If inDegree[v] becomes 0, enqueue v.","5. If completedCount === numCourses return true; else return false (cycle exists)."],"alternatives":["DFS 3-color: State 0 (unvisited), State 1 (visiting / on current recursion stack), State 2 (visited). If DFS encounters a State 1 node, a back-edge / cycle is detected."]}},{"stageId":"course-decisions","stageKind":"decisions","learnerPrompt":"Why is Kahn\'s algorithm generally preferred over DFS in production build systems (like Make, Bazel, or Webpack)?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"tradeOffs":["Stack overflow safety: Kahn\'s algorithm is iterative (queue-based), avoiding recursion stack overflow on deep graphs (e.g. 100,000 tasks in a pipeline).","Parallel execution: In build systems (Bazel/Make), all nodes with inDegree === 0 in the queue can be compiled in parallel across multiple worker threads simultaneously!"]}},{"stageId":"course-scale-failure","stageKind":"scale-failure","learnerPrompt":"Scale challenge: In a monorepo with 50,000 micro-packages and 500,000 dependency edges, a developer introduces a circular dependency in a PR. How does CI pinpoint the exact cycle path?","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"failureModes":["Kahn\'s tells if a cycle exists, but not the cycle path: Kahn\'s returns false when queue empties early, leaving remaining nodes with inDegree > 0.","Cycle path extraction: Run DFS starting from any remaining unvisited node with inDegree > 0. Follow edges until a node in the current recursion stack is revisited, extracting the exact cycle path (e.g. auth-service -> user-service -> auth-service) to show in the CI error log."]}},{"stageId":"course-followup","stageKind":"follow-up","learnerPrompt":"Course Schedule II: Instead of just returning true/false, return an actual valid ordering of all courses. If impossible, return an empty array.","available":true,"relatedConceptSlugs":["database-indexing"],"referenceMaterial":{"summary":"Topological order collection.","approachSteps":["1. Append each dequeued course u to an order array.","2. If order.length === numCourses, return order; else return []."]}}],"supportingResourceIds":[]}]');function r(){return i.filter(e=>"canonical"===e.reviewState)}function n(e){let t=e.toLowerCase();return r().filter(e=>e.conceptSlugs.some(e=>e.toLowerCase()===t))}let s=["understand","attempt","solution","decisions","scale-failure","follow-up"];function o(e){return[...e].sort((e,t)=>s.indexOf(e.stageKind)-s.indexOf(t.stageKind))}function l(e){var t;return null!==(t=({understand:"Understand",attempt:"Your Approach",solution:"Reference Solution",decisions:"Decisions & Trade-offs","scale-failure":"Scale & Failure","follow-up":"Follow-up"})[e])&&void 0!==t?t:e}}}]);